A facade can re-export declarations from another source file or package without
defining a wrapper. Place `$export` immediately before a direct-selection
dependency clause in `@mod.moth` or `@package.moth`. Add one `@route` operand to
publish the selected names under a public route instead of the facade root.

### Direct-selection re-exports

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- library/@mod.moth
    $export
    @components/card CardData as Card, render_card

    $export @forms/numbers
    @core/math PI, sin
]

This publishes `Card` and `render_card` at the facade root and `PI` and `sin`
under the nested `forms/numbers` route, reached as `@library/forms/numbers PI`.
`CardData` remains available under its original name only if the facade also
selects it without an alias. A leaf `as` alias is both the local selected name
and the public name.

One directive applies to all names on the following clause. An ordinary
unmarked dependency clause stays private:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @components/card CardData as LocalCard
]

`LocalCard` is now bound in this file, but is not public.

### Route ownership

The route operand belongs to the exporting facade. It is not a path into the
provider and not the provider's own route. The selection after the clause still
names the provider surface:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- library/@mod.moth
    $export @mirror
    @provider/nested widget as widget_label
]

The provider publishes `widget` under its own `nested` route. It is a child
module of `library`, so `@provider/nested` resolves from the `library` module
root. The facade selects it through that path. Its public path is
`@library/mirror`, so consumers write `@library/mirror widget_label`.
`widget_label` is the public leaf because the alias names it. A provider route
is never inherited into the re-export path.

A route never renames anything. Explicit selections and `as` aliases decide
which declarations cross the facade and what each public leaf is called. Two
`$export` directives on one clause are a duplicate even when their routes
differ, because one target has one public destination.

### Selection syntax

The route operand stays on the `$export` line. The provider path and first
selected name must begin on the same physical line after it. A comma continues
the list across a newline:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $export @forms
    @components/card CardData,
        render_card
]

Without a comma, a newline ends the clause. A trailing comma is invalid because another selected name is required.

Re-exports require a nonempty list of flat, explicit selections. Whole namespaces, namespace aliases, nested selections, wildcard exports and export lists are unsupported. A route operand does not add nested selection syntax. Receiver methods remain attached to their receiver type and cannot be re-exported as separate selected names.

### Public identity and validation

Re-exports preserve the selected declaration's origin, type and callable error
contract through any number of hops. The publishing facade owns the binding. A
route changes where that binding appears, not its origin identity.

The public identity is `(route, leaf)`. Distinct names in one route merge into
one public namespace. Two selected names that resolve to the same leaf in one
route report `MOTH-RULE-0081` at both sites. The same leaf in different routes
is legal when it comes from one selected target.

That limit follows from local binding. A routed re-export still binds its public
leaf as a local name in the facade, so two different targets that both use
`widget` collide as local names (`MOTH-IMPORT-0009`) even when their routes
differ. Two `$export` directives on one clause, even with different routes, are
a duplicate directive diagnostic with both sites.

Any two distinct route spellings in one facade must differ by more than ASCII
case. `@forms` and `@Forms` report `MOTH-RULE-0092` at both operands. A route
that overlaps a consumer-visible child or source key or a registered source or
binding package prefix reports `MOTH-IMPORT-0006` eagerly, even when no consumer
references it. A case-only match counts as a collision.

Two facades that share one visible key create an ambiguous base. Identical route
claims from different facades behind that base stay latent, and the base and
each such route report `MOTH-IMPORT-0006` on use. A route behind an ambiguous
base that overlaps a different visible provider, such as a selectable child
facade or one of its routes, a local source or a registered package prefix, is
an eager `MOTH-IMPORT-0006` collision even when unused.

Exported local names must be unique across direct declarations and re-exported
selections in one facade. A re-export cannot expose an unknown or inaccessible
private declaration.

A private file or directory in the exporting module may share a route spelling
and stays private. An undeclared route, a deeper route that the facade never
published or a leaf missing from a declared route reports an import diagnostic.
It never falls through to a private path.

Receiver methods stay attached to their receiver type. A routed type carries its
methods for callers, but a method is not a selectable leaf and cannot be
re-exported separately (`MOTH-IMPORT-0011`).

A private callable's inferred implicit failure cannot escape through a public
callable without an appropriate declared error slot or local handling. See
@../project-structure/#public-api (Public API) for the public contract.

